			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825505">
									

<p>Thank you for the clarification. Now that I'm clear on your intentions, I can offer a bit of crit.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Collapsing&nbsp;this&nbsp;as&nbsp;to&nbsp;save&nbsp;space</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">fwoop</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I want to note that, in many ways, the subject matter is still uncomfortable. Even as I now understand your desire to make this an anti-harassment story, an anomaly based on the objectification of a woman's breasts is practically a minefield for things to go wrong.</p>
<p>I don't mean to say that you <em>can't</em> write this in a way that is respectful, it's just moreso to do with the subject matter itself. Sexual harassment, in general, is a dubious<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-113826-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-113826-1')">1</a></sup> topic to write about. Once again, your intentions are essentially irrelevant if you can't articulate them in a way that gets them across clearly. (In this case, misinterpretation is a killer.)</p>
<p>Now, I'd like to touch on the actual story itself, setting aside my previous concerns for a moment. In particular, I'd like to get a bit more insight into the structure. I largely hold that the way you portray your narrative holds just as much, if not more importance than the narrative itself. (See also: the initial points in this posts. They link together quite well.)</p>
<p>From my understanding, there is an initial incident regarding Dr. Briggs that ends in his blindness. Following that, experimentation on Lopez. I understand that the initial incident serves the narrative purpose of introducing the anomaly while also giving the &quot;pervert gets what he deserves&quot; point, which is fine. I'm still largely unclear on the experiments, which really should serve some sort of narrative purpose. Are they going to make a nuanced point on anti-harassment? Or are they just going to serve as an explanation for how the anomaly works? I should note that the latter generally just&#8230; isn't interesting. A reader could care less about the inner semantics of an anomaly. An SCP article is a narrative that follows a specific format - if you end a story on &quot;and THIS is how is all happened&#8230;&quot;, it's generally going to feel weak.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>based on an interview with Lopez's parents, it is speculated that it might have first come about during puberty.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems to be another addition? Many of my points above apply here - what do you think this would add, would the reader really care about this? Would its inclusion benefit the article, or would it only serve as unnecessary bloat? As an aside, what do you hope to achieve with this point? Is it a subtle protest against the objectification of young women? It might be the lack of context, but the point as it stands is just&#8230; creepy.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
