<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there DoctorDeeDeeDee. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<p>So yeah, I’m gonna echo a few of <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/naveil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5986482); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5986482&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606133763" alt="Naveil" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5986482)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/naveil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5986482); return false;">Naveil</a></span>’s points here before adding my own take. I feel the subject matter is uncomfortable, but maybe it’s because I’m of Dr. Briggs’ gender and I couldn’t possibly empathise, or maybe the narrative is designed to be uncomfortable to some extent. I think there’s no easy way to traverse the subject matter, and the best way would be to convey your true emotions / thoughts / feelings towards the situation. What I mean by this is steer the hell away from comedy if, as you’ve claimed, these kind of situations did not make you laugh before (and they really shouldn’t). Instead, the tone of your article should mirror your feels of indignation and horror towards people like Briggs.</p>
<p>Personally, I’m not too sure as well about the rest of the article. That grotesque description of what happens to Briggs will serve well as a discovery log, but what else is there in the rest of the article? I think you need to clarify the anomaly’s origins in order to know where you’re going next. For example, if you’re suggesting that the scp is somehow an entity within Dr. Lopez, how did it end up there? What are its goals? Is it working alone or are there are others out there in society (hint: links to the #MeToo movement, for example) that are also taking down men like Briggs? And then, you can ask yourself how the Foundation plans to deal with it? It won’t be a Safe anomaly any longer then.</p>
<p>There are, of course, other ways you can play this. For example, do you need Lopez and the entity to be separate? You mentioned in an earlier post that the anomaly was discovered sometime during puberty? Why not make Lopez into the scp herself, then she somehow possesses powers to manipulate and harm others who sexually idolise her. I’d initially thought this was the direction you were going for because you said, “People frequently wondered why Dr. Jenna Lopez never seemed to mind that Dr. Briggs kept staring at her chest,” and I was asking myself <em>why</em> she wouldn’t she mind? Is she aware of the entity within her? Are you suggesting that she’d personally <em>allowed</em> Briggs to get away with sexual harassment because she knows he’d eventually get punished by the entity? Do you see how this could also be problematic for your message?</p>
<p>All in all, I feel this has plenty of potential, just that you’ll need to work doubly hard in order to tread this minefield. That being said, I think as long as you’re clear about your intentions, we’re willing to work with you such that your eventual narrative reflects that to the best of your ability. If you decide to revise your concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to reply. All the best for now!</p>

